! Ferromagnetic fcc nickel, the magnon case -- and the one norm-conserving 3d
! ferromagnet this repository has a dataset for.
!
! Ni.pbe-nc-sg15.UPF carries 18 valence electrons (3s and 3p as semicore), so
! this is far from the cheapest cell here; it is used because the transverse
! susceptibility's matrix elements are the plane-wave ones, which an ultrasoft
! or PAW dataset invalidates, and every other magnetic dataset committed here
! is one of those.
!
! The 4x4x4 grid is the compromise the cost forces and it bounds what can be
! claimed: the smallest wavevector it offers along Gamma-X is already 0.89
! inverse angstrom, where nickel's magnon is a few hundred meV and is
! Landau-damped by the Stoner continuum. It is enough to show the collective
! mode separating from that continuum and to measure the Goldstone residual;
! it is not a converged spin-wave stiffness.
!
! nosym and noinv with the whole grid, for the reason h-fcc-magnon.in gives.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 6.65, nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 60.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.02,
    nspin = 2
    nosym = .true., noinv = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.3
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-9
    mixing_beta = 0.3
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Ni 58.69 Ni.pbe-nc-sg15.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Ni 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 4 4 4 0 0 0
